<article class="env-remark" id="09SD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09SD">Remark <span data-tag="09SD">22.37.9</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $A$ and $B$ be $R$-algebras. If $D(A)$ and $D(B)$ are equivalent as $R$-linear triangulated categories, then the centers of $A$ and $B$ are isomorphic as $R$-algebras. In particular, if $A$ and $B$ are commutative, then $A \cong B$. The rather tricky proof can be found in <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Proposition 9.2</span>, <a href="/bibliography/Rickard">Rickard</a>]</span> or <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Proposition 6.3.2</span>, <a href="/bibliography/KZ">KZ</a>]</span>. Another approach might be to use Hochschild cohomology (see remark below). </p>
</article>